A correct original answer can survive an irrelevant change while failing to respond to a change in meaning. We selected 32 distinct BoolQ paragraphs with an original question and an independently authored edit that flips the publisher's answer [10, 11]. We randomized the 64 requests. Each arm used the identical passage, thinking-enabled prompt and 384-token limit. A whole-pair success requires two correct completed free generations. A second endpoint compares the full model's yes and no logits at the same post-thinking prefix, before either model has supplied an answer. No example from the publisher contrast set contributed to training. The original backbone's pretraining corpus is unavailable for contamination testing.

#figure(image("human-reading.svg",width: 92%),caption: [Free answers and paired counterfactual accuracy on publisher-edited questions. The probe fixes the answer position for both arms.])

The first model repeated the final native attention layer twice and trained 802,816 adapter parameters for 750 eight-example updates on 1,536 balanced BoolQ training passages. Both arms completed all 64 questions. The adapted model gave 41 correct answers against 43 from Base. It won four complete passage pairs and lost six (exact paired $p=0.754$). The shared-prefix probe also favored Base, 48 correct versus 41. The candidate generated 256 tokens in 8.29 s; Base generated 8,970 tokens in 133.93 s. Probe evaluation took 4.76 and 4.34 s, respectively. The experiment demonstrates that teaching a compact response can reduce time substantially while weakening a model's underlying reading judgments. Its preregistered decision closed promotion for this fit.

A second fit started from the instruction sibling. Each eight-example batch combined four naturally occurring BoolQ questions and two pairs of opposite e-SNLI hypotheses about the same premise. A margin loss on the two answer logits supplemented response likelihood. After 1,000 updates over 2,048 BoolQ passages and 512 e-SNLI pairs, the model completed all 64 questions from the other 32 contrast-set passages. It answered 46/64 correctly versus 50/64 for Base, including both questions on 15 versus 19 passages (five paired gains, nine losses, $p=0.424$). Shared-prefix scores were 44 versus 47. Again, the large latency advantage, 6.84 versus 142.84 s, accompanied lower reading accuracy. The two independent passage panels give the same direction of effect despite different initializations and objectives.

A later lexical audit found one near-duplicate training paragraph on each reading panel. Their word five-gram Jaccard similarities were 0.875 and 0.831. In the first case replacing numbers with one common symbol made the two normalized paragraphs identical. The evaluated questions differed. Removing the flagged passage and its two questions from each panel gave 10 versus 12 wholly correct pairs among the first 31 passages, and 14 versus 19 among the other 31. These post hoc sensitivity counts supplement the original unfiltered decisions.

*Natural two-hop questions.* On HotpotQA we retained the question and all ten original paragraphs for each of 40 held-out bridge questions [15]. The fit used 384 training questions with disjoint supporting titles. A final-layer answer adapter learned the training answers; two applications of native layer 15 learned document retrieval from publisher support labels. The predicted terminal document received a fixed attention bias during answer generation. Both arms retained every input token. Neither had access to the evaluation support labels.

The model selected both supporting documents on 3/40 questions, one on 20, and neither on 17. It supplied the reference phrase on 11/40 against 20/40 for Base (two paired gains, eleven losses, two-sided $p=0.0225$). Normalized exact match was 9 versus 16 and mean token F1 was 0.270 versus 0.529. With neither supporting document selected, it still answered four questions correctly by the phrase metric. Conversely, two of the three correctly retrieved pairs produced a wrong answer. The shared reader used 41.40 s and 2.48 GiB; Base used 120.08 s and 1.68 GiB. The registered evidence and answer thresholds both failed.

A subsequent study tested a reader that can follow directed relations among the actual passages. Earlier multi-hop dense retrieval work uses evidence from one retrieval step to condition the next [26]. Our method learned a distribution over the first document, then used it to weight title and passage relations before applying the native layer again. The same frozen short-answer adapter receives a learned attention focus. Every answer token is generated with access to all ten original documents.

On 48 new human two-hop questions, this reader retrieved both annotated documents on 23, one below the registered floor of 24. It answered 17 exactly versus 15 for Base. On the primary reference-content scorer it recorded 18 versus 19, with nine gains and ten losses (two-sided paired $p=1$). Mean token F1 was 0.451 versus 0.478. It completed 48 answers versus 47, while reducing end-to-end time from 132.05 to 26.69 s and peak allocation from 1.68 to 1.11 GiB. Aggregate first-token time increased from 21.89 to 23.65 s. When asked which streaming service adapted the film _Snatch_, the reader answered “Crackle” and Base supplied the title of the series. Asked for the former stadium name used by the Aztecs, it supplied “SDCCU Stadium” while Base correctly supplied “Qualcomm Stadium”. Each answer was decoded freely. The primary quality and first-token conditions closed this reader.

*Learning when to copy from a source.* A separate two-pass model starts from untouched Base and learns its own answer decoder on 2,048 human reading questions, half from SQuAD and half from the first turn of QuAC [29, 30]. It uses the same adapted native layer 23 at both passes. At each output position the model allocates a learned fraction of probability to positions in the user-supplied passage. Occurrences of the same token contribute to the same vocabulary entry. The remaining probability comes from the complete native vocabulary, including tokens absent from the passage. Pointer-generator networks previously used a learned generate/copy mixture for summarization [28]. The 96-dimensional query and key projections here use contextual Qwen states. A learned coefficient can favor source positions whose predecessor matches the most recently observed token. Training uses the publisher's span for an auxiliary alignment loss. Inference receives the question and complete passage.

The separate 64-question development panel spans eight previously unused SQuAD articles and 32 QuAC articles. The QuAC subset contains 24 answerable questions and eight judged unanswerable by a majority of annotators. We removed QuAC's appended no-answer marker before tokenization. Both models receive the same request, thinking-enabled chat prefix and 384-token greedy budget. We score all enrolled questions against the accepted human references and report completion, exact match, content and token F1. One question, “where was he born?”, also occurs in training for different people. The held-out subject is Roy Acuff. No passage duplicates a fitting passage, and the maximum passage-level five-word Jaccard similarity after masking numbers is 0.019. A per-item paired test on this panel does not imply 64 independent articles.


#figure(image("passage-answers.svg",width:93%), caption:[Exact completed answers and token-level overlap with the accepted human references. Unanswerable items are a subset of QuAC. Each mark uses the full enrolled denominator.])

Training prepared frozen features in 219.18 s and optimized 1,402,050 parameters in 396.51 s. Of the 2,048 source documents, 1,941 had a usable publisher span for the auxiliary supervision. Both models completed all 64 questions. The reader gave 13 exact answers versus 16 for Base (six gains, nine losses, item-paired $p=0.607$). Swapping model identities jointly within each of the 40 source articles gives a two-sided exact $p=0.508$. Reference content fell from 34 answers to 21 and mean token F1 from 0.495 to 0.419. QuAC F1 rose from 0.365 to 0.385, while exact answers fell from five to four. Both models answered four of eight unanswerable questions exactly. The reader also abstained on six answerable questions.

The reader used 663 generated tokens and 17.73 s versus 9,435 tokens and 145.60 s for Base. Peak allocation fell from 1.69 to 1.12 GiB; aggregate first-token time rose from 10.20 to 11.22 s. All six incremental probes passed. On the 471 generated token decisions other than the closing thinking tag, double newline and turn boundary, the median learned copy probability was 0.00033, and only 14 had a value above one half. The reader correctly supplied 1804 for the year of Joseph Willard's death where Base abstained. It gave “1299” when asked which European visited China and returned, while Base gave the accepted “Marco Polo”. These results closed the reading model's release and matched-depth successor.

*Semantic alignment and transfer to actual support questions.* Two models in the earlier entailment depth comparison each answered 85/120. We tested a different mechanism that explicitly compares contextual representations of two user-supplied texts before applying the final native attention layer twice. Bidirectional soft attention aligns tokens. Aligned states, their differences and products, and the expected displacement of matching tokens pass through learned projections and pooling, following prior work on local sentence comparison [33]. A learned continuous vector enters the decoder only at the final prompt position and subsequent output positions. Both passes use the same rank-32 adaptations. Both models freely generate their answers. An independent probe compares yes and no at an identical answer prefix.

Training uses 1,536 PAWS-Wiki anchor sentences, each with a human-judged paraphrase and a high-overlap alternative with a different meaning [31]. The objective combines answer-token likelihood with a margin between the two judgments. Evaluation contains 24 different Wikipedia anchors and 16 genuine AskUbuntu support questions. For each support question the relevant and irrelevant alternatives are the highest-ranked eligible BM25 candidates with those human judgments [32]. The model has no AskUbuntu supervision. It receives both titles and complete bodies from the published file. The publisher README describes 100-word bodies. We found a 3,734-word body in the file, and retained all of it. The resulting request contains 12,518 tokens. The other support requests and all Wikipedia comparisons also retain their full text. Among 120 distinct held-out texts the largest five-word Jaccard similarity to any fitting text is 0.25. None of the support posts names its paired post ID or contains the screened explicit duplicate markers. Balanced positive and negative judgments within each anchor make constant yes or no insufficient for a complete pair.

#figure(image("meaning-pairs.svg",width:94%),caption:[Fraction of anchors on which the model judged both texts correctly. The independent probe fixes the answer position and compares the native yes and no logits.])

The fit prepared frozen features in 88.49 s and optimized 1,018,177 parameters in 92.61 s. On Wikipedia, free judgments were correct on 44/48 versus 5/48 for Base, including both alternatives for 20/24 anchors versus none. All 20 discordant anchors favored the candidate (exact two-sided $p=0.00000191$). The common-prefix probe reduced the gap to 20 versus 13 complete anchors and 44 versus 36 individual judgments. This separates improved semantic discrimination on the source task from better adherence to the requested answer format.

On the 16 AskUbuntu anchors, neither model judged both alternatives correctly even with the common answer prefix. The candidate answered “no” on every support comparison, getting all 16 irrelevant alternatives and missing all 16 relevant ones. For example, it distinguished a Cedar Trail driver installation question from a DKMS installation problem but also rejected a related report about a DKMS update that ran indefinitely. The transfer changes the subject matter, text lengths and relation of interest: two questions can benefit from the same solution while describing different experiences. The experiment does not isolate which change explains the failure.

Across all 80 requests the candidate gave 60 correct judgments versus seven, completed 80 versus 61, and took 16.13 versus 346.43 s. Peak allocation was 1.48 versus 1.96 GiB. Summed first-token latency increased from 12.43 to 13.57 s. A broad claim about understanding real support questions would conflict with both endpoints on that source. The registered transfer requirement closed promotion and the matched-depth successor.
